  "notes": "Eastern Punjabi (Gurmukhi script, India). Typologically remarkable: lexical tone developed from the loss of the Old Indo-Aryan voiced-aspirate (murmured) series. ⟨ਘ ਝ ਢ ਧ ਭ⟩ carry that tonal reflex; word-initially they merged with the voiceless unaspirated series (*DH > T) and elsewhere with the plain voiced one (*DH > D), which the PA_DH_INITIAL_* rules encode (Kanwal & Ritchart 2015; Evans, Yeh & Kulkarni 2018). The pitch target is contested in the literature — low in Bhatia and Shackle, falling in the recent acoustic studies — and the spec keeps the low-tone reading. ⟨ਹ⟩ triggers low tone on an adjacent vowel when not initial; that context is not yet encoded. The non-initial *DH reflex has its own unencoded gap: the gold data (e.g. ਅਠਵੰਝਾ, ਅੱਧ) puts a high/rising tone on the vowel PRECEDING a non-initial ⟨ਘ ਝ ਢ ਧ ਭ⟩, not on the consonant itself, matching Kanwal & Ritchart's restriction of the word-initial reflex to word-initial position; the PA_DH_INITIAL_* rules only cover the word-initial case and non-initial ⟨ਘ ਝ ਢ ਧ ਭ⟩ currently surface voiced with no tone mark at all. Being an abugida, an unmarked consonant letter carries the inherent ⟨ə⟩, but the schwa following the last consonant of a word is elided (PA_SCHWA_FINAL_*, Singh & Lehal). Medial schwa deletion is context-sensitive well beyond what the rule layer expresses and is deliberately not attempted. The addak ⟨ੱ⟩ geminates the FOLLOWING consonant; the engine has no gemination-mark mechanism, so the length mark currently lands on the preceding vowel. Western Punjabi (Shahmukhi, Pakistan) is written in Perso-Arabic script.",
  "quality": "research",
  "script_type": "abugida",
  "inherent_vowel": "ə",
