      "surface": "k˩ə",
      "word_initial": true,
      "notes": "Word-initial reflex of the historical voiced aspirate (murmured) series ⟨ਘ ਝ ਢ ਧ ਭ⟩: word-initially it merged with the voiceless unaspirated series, non-initially with the plain voiced one. Kanwal & Ritchart 2015, 'An experimental investigation of tonogenesis in Punjabi', ICPhS 18 (abstract: 'we confirm that these consonants are now realised as unaspirated, and, word-initially, also as voiceless'); Evans, Yeh & Kulkarni 2018, Transactions of the Philological Society 116(3): 509-528, doi:10.1111/1467-968X.12135, cited here via secondary summaries for the same word-initial merger and pitch-target survey — the article sits behind a paywall and was not verified against the full text, so no page or direct quote is given. The tone letter stays on the segment for the word-initial case covered here, where both studies locate the reflex on the following vowel within the same onset-consonant's syllable; neither source supports that placement for the non-initial reflex, which the spec-level note records separately. The pitch TARGET is contested (low in Bhatia 1975/1993, Gill 1996, Shackle 2003; falling in Bowden 2012, Kanwal & Ritchart 2015, and as further surveyed in Evans et al. 2018), and the spec keeps the low-tone reading."
